speechtext:
I might also, Mr. Chairman, call your attention to the following, in the journals of the House of Commons, of the 28tli March, 1870, volume 3, page 116:
The orders of the day being read, for the second reading of the Bill to amend the Acts of incorporation of the Great Western Railway Company;
The Hon. Mr. Carling moved, seconded by Mr. Shanly, and the question being proposed, that the Bill be now read a second time;
And objection being taken by Mr. Cameron, member for the electoral district of the south riding of the county of Huron, that certain of the provisions of the said Bill as amended by the Select Standing Committee on Railways, Canals and Telegraph Lines were not contemplated in the notice given under the rules of the House, nor in the petition praying for the passing of said Bill;
Mr. Speaker decided that the Bill should be referred to the Select Standing Committee on Standing Orders to report whether the powers to be conferred are in excess of the notice given, or whether they are substantially included in it.
On motion of the Hon. Mr. Carling, seconded by Mr. Shanly,
Ordered, that the said order be discharged; and the Bill be referred to the Select Standing Committee on Standing Orders.
This is a case identically in point, in which the Committee on Railways and Canals made an amendment to a Bill that was not contemplated in the notice. This amendment, changing the denomination of the shares from $400 to $100, goes to the very essence of the constitution of the company and affects the voting power of the different shareholders. Consequently I submit that we should not proceed with the Bill until notice of this amendment is published, so that the parties affected may be informed that such a change is proposed to be made in their vested rights.
